^^Adjectives and adverbs^^

Adjectives are words that describe a noun, such as the words *kaunis* (beautiful) and *kirkas* (bright), e.g. *aurinko on kirkas* (the sun is bright), that you learned in Unit 0.
Adverbs are like adjectives but for verbs, such as *kauniisti* (beautifully) and *kirkkaasti* (brightly), e.g. *aurinko paistaa kirkkaasti* (the sun is shining brightly). In other words, words that answer questions like where, when, how etc. are adverbs.

As you see, when making an adverb you take the adjective and modify it to become an adverb, usually by putting *-sti* at the end (like in English bright+ly, beautiful+ly etc.) but also here the word stem changes a bit. Now, let's dive into why the word stem changes in certain words but not others.


^Why did *kaunis* get an extra 'i' when adding *-sti* to the end? And why did *kirkas* receive both a second 'k' and an 'i' if we added the exact same ending to both words?^

We touched upon this a little bit in the 'Countries and nationalities' -section and the answer to this is complicated, but it has to do with the fact that when adding an ending, we don't *only* add the ending. We actually modify the word to another case (in this case into the genitive) and *then* add the ending, but you don't see it very well from the end result, that's why we left it unexplained until now. Finnish textbooks and language courses usually have you learn words together with some of their cases, like the following words: 

sun = "aurinko, auringon, aurinkoa, aurinkoja"
bright = "kirkas, kirkkaan, kirkasta, kirkkaita"
beautiful = "kaunis, kauniin, kaunista, kauniita"

The reason why words are taught like this in schools is because knowing at least some words and their declensions by heart also helps the learner know which words change when adding an ending and how. A learner who has learned the word *kirkas* by heart like in the table above, would know instantly how to make it into an adverb: you take the second form (the genitive) of *kirkas*, i.e. *kirkkaan* and remove the 'n', add the *-sti* ending to that form, resulting in *kirkkaasti*, the adverb.


^Why does *kirkas* need another 'k'? Why can't it just be *kirkaan*?^

The question is etymological in nature and therefore hard to answer. There is something in Finnish called the KPT-change, or 'Consonant Gradation', and it's a rule which states that certain consonants receive certain changes depending on which word group the word in question belongs to. Consonant Gradation has a ton of rules and even more exceptions to those rules. Instead of trying to teach you more grammar tables, we are going to do a more reasonable thing and let you learn words, from now on, with the nominative and the genitive together, like *kirkas, kirkkaan*, *kirja, kirjan*, which will help you know how the word changes when adding a suffix to it. 

As a langauge learning app, Lingonaut is well suited for teaching words and grammar in context, and some things you will learn better by not immediately caring about the rules and instead learning phrases, but in this case we hugely recommend checking out the following [link]. 

^As always, in language learning it is still very much recommended to use more resources than one, even if that resource is Lingonaut.^